what may look like a pile of rubbish to  the rest of us is a treasure trove for  Katya Varna she transforms discarded  bicycle tubing into something new and  exciting it's really a great material  it's water-resistant its airtight its  durable and can still be used for so  many different things it would be a  shame just to burn it Katja gives new  life to junk that would otherwise go up  in smoke in scrap yards like an iPad  case or a handbag all Koch's products  are handmade at her shop in Berlin she  gets the inner tubing also for  motorbikes and trucks and tire and bike  shops size shape and texture dictate its  eventual use it's often hard to tell  where the creative process will lead me  first I have to get a feel for the  material I may start with a rough idea  but that often gets discarded when  something more exciting occurs to me and  I end up with an entirely different  product Koch has been working with  rubber tubing now for six years she  first trained as a graphic designer  so she had to learn these techniques  luckily her material was never out of  reach I ride bikes and motorbikes so I'm  familiar with rubber tubing I started  experimenting with the material and  thought wow you can make practical  everyday objects out of it as well as  seals and patches my first product was a  keyring filled with air so if your keys  fell into water they wouldn't sink  and many products have followed how  about a case for glasses made from  motorbike tires under her KWD label  kochia also offers all kinds of  different bags from purses to large hand  bags the unique designs are customized  with tire treads patches valves and even  reflectors you sometimes get a few snide  remarks making money from trash but some  say what a brilliant idea what surprises  me is that it's usually older people who  are most impressed there's somebody  who's making something useful out of  that material that's great the designer  sells her accessories from her Berlin  shop and all across the world over the  Internet small bags cost between 20 and  35 euros belt bags are also on offer  it's a perfect fit  Koch's bags are evidence of an upcycling  trend transforming used objects back  into something valuable as a designer I  have responsibilities and I'm an  environmentally conscious person but  that's not the driving factor what  drives me first and foremost as a  designer is the encounter with and  transformation of the material and it  keeps on inspiring new objects whether  it's furniture or everyday objects like  a fruit bowl  this summer kochia is taking part in her  first joint exhibition in Berlin she'll  be showing some very different creations  made out of rubbish  Pecha finds inspiration when she's out  riding her bicycle I get lots of ideas  out on my bike it's important to look  around you at everything in the world  critically and that inspires new  projects catchy burner is not trying to  reinvent the wheel but whenever she  takes one apart she makes something new
 

